#{[#edb/ref 17592186088227 "The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+3x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)-9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)-9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -6\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =0$ and $\\beta =-1.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186088393 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =1$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta -4\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-2\\alpha -4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -4\\beta -2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},$ $\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186088395 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{2i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$ \n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ -4\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186092884 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-5y=0 \\\\ x=-2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 0-(-5)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186092937 "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z=-1 \\\\ y+4z=1 \\\\ 5x+6y=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-24)-2(-20)+3(-5) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & -20 & -5 \\\\ -18 & -15 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 20 & -5 \\\\ 18 & -15 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 20 & -15 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}42 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=42,y=-35,z=9.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186115063 "First, we organize the system so that it’s in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3x-5y=0 \\\\ x-y=-8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n![](images/64/648952bc6e093c08ff29215fce21fe115ab81e9fc3d8b423772123eae31237f3.png)\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n![](images/c1/c1f203be0770c298d9c2c75ccfbfb30354cb314ed75c8b05f6639e5633ca7442.png)\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n![](images/aa/aa4de061ca95088763e7e5fec289dbd2e5bd298e8986ef6660617ea0ba227821.png)\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ becomes\n\n![](images/75/75aed8c54a2ec274def967802cbd3b67d0fad3358e676b02cf52e1e8d2cbe0d7.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186115064 "The  $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}10x-15y=5 \\\\ 1x+0y=-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y=1 \\\\ x=-2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186115733 "The  $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-5y=10 \\\\ x+0y=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-5y=10 \\\\ x=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186120269 "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,0,5)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,0,5⟩ \\\\ ⟨t+1,\\,8\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,2{t}^{2}+3⟩ & =⟨2,0,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t+1=2 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{ln}⁡t=0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+3=5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,0,5)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{8}{t},\\,4t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{8}{1},\\,4(1)⟩=⟨1,8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+64+16} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2,0,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨1,8,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\frac{8}{9},\\frac{4}{9}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186124817 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y+4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+4=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(2\\lambda -1)=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-1),(0,1).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2(1)-1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-1),\\,(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-1)$ | $(0,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,1)$ and its maximum value is $9.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186125037 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2xy=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=R.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the entire segment of solutions ${(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,3]}.$ Since all these points do not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include them in the list of candidates for extrema (they lie on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider them in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(3,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,3).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)^{2}(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,3).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,3)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,3).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)^{2}(t) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,3).$\n\n- The quarter-circle connecting $(3,0)$ and $(0,3)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}},t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}},t) \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}})}^{2}\\cdot t \\\\  & =9t-{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,3),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (9t-{t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 9-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(3-{t}^{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=\\sqrt{3},$ inside the interval $t\\in (0,3).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one  candidate point, $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{3}).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(3,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(3,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the points $(0,0),\\,(3,0),(0,3),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $6\\sqrt{3}$ at the point $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{3}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186125064 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+6y & 2y+6x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y=0 \\\\ 2y+6x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+6(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2 \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2 \\\\  & =4+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{2},0),\\,(1,1),\\,(-1,1),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(1,1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(-1,-1)$ | $(1,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $10$ | $-2$ | $10$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $10$ at the points $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186125065 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y-1 & x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y-1=0 \\\\ x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,1).$ Since the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)(0)-(t) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)(t)-(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(1,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,0⟩+t(⟨0,2⟩-⟨1,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩+t⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(1-t,2t) \\\\  & =(1-t)(2t)-(1-t) \\\\  & =-2{t}^{2}+3t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (-2{t}^{2}+3t-1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -4t+3 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one candidate point, $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $\\frac{1}{8}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186125139 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A)=-12+3=-9.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186147772 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{x})({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{2x})^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ (-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{e}^{5x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186147775 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{x})({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}){e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{4x}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186151969 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2t+6.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\ny_p(t) = \\alpha t + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' =  \\alpha,\n\\qquad y_p'' = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}(0)-4t(\\alpha )+6(\\alpha t+\\beta ) & =2t+6 \\\\ (-4\\alpha +6\\alpha )t+6\\beta  & =2t+6 \\\\ 2\\alpha t+6\\beta  & =2t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,t) \\\\ 6\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = 1$ and $\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = t + 1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186151972 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x=e^t,$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x=e^t,$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm d t} = e^t = x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac1x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}+y=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous ODE equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing.\n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A\\cos t+B\\sin t.\n$$\n\nSince right-hand side of the inhomogeneous ODE is a polynomial of degree $1,$ we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+ \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\alpha\n,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}t^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n0+(\\alpha t+\\beta)=t\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of [math]t[/math]}) \\\\ \\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has the solution $\\alpha=1$ and $\\beta=0.$ \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = t.\n$$\n\nNow, all that remains is to write the above solution in terms of the original variable $x.$ Since $x=e^t,$ we have $t = \\ln x,$ and therefore\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =\\ln x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186152621 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+4y=4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{2t}+Bt{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha +4(\\alpha t+\\beta )=4t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n4\\alpha t+4(\\beta -\\alpha )=4t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ \\beta -\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the constants}).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =1,$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186152625 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-6x+1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are the constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(0)-4x(\\alpha )+6(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =-6x+1 \\\\ (-4\\alpha +6\\alpha )x+6\\beta  & =-6x+1 \\\\ 2\\alpha x+6\\beta  & =-6x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 6\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3$ and $\\beta =\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x+\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186152626 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and sinusoidal forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+B{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (-\\alpha -4\\beta +3\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-\\beta +4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (2\\alpha -4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha -4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =1,$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186172385 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+2y-6z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2x-3y+2z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2,-6⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2,-3,2⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 2=-3\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ -6=2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{7}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{7}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{49}{4{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{49}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{49}{2{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+2z & =0 \\\\ 2(1)-3(-1)+2z & =0 \\\\ 5+2z & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+2z & =0 \\\\ 2(-1)-3(1)+2z & =0 \\\\ -5+2z & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,-1,-\\frac{5}{2}),\\,(-1,1,\\frac{5}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,-1,-\\frac{5}{2})$ | $(-1,1,\\frac{5}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $14$ | $-14$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,-1,-\\frac{5}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $14.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186221150 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=4t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ r(4t)={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t\\alpha_1 = e^{4t} - e^{t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{3t}\\bigl(e^{4t}-e^{t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3\\alpha_0 = 4e^{t} - e^{4t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigl(4e^{t}-e^{4t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}+4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t} \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{4t} \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((4{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{t})+(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/10/104a4830d44821dbc65ad9c832da0323b7dd9e90c934fdfc7a21c175845231d2.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186221151 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= (3+2\\textrm{i}) t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2= (3-2\\textrm{i}) t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((3+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(3+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(3+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/de/dec5e32e503bf178832f6cf08b51767d2f76fb308e9a9d07c4e805389507bbfd.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186221152 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(6t)={e}^{6t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 = e^{6t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n6t e^{6t} + \\alpha_0 = e^{6t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = e^{6t}(1-6t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =8{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1+2t), \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(-2t), \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(2t), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/70/70c4e625ac52b04e9a1fb258470138050a51cdacaced8714cd2a313782055c3f.png)"] [#edb/ref 17592186221925 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t)={e}^{-4t} \\\\ r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t} \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-5t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second equation by $4$ and adding to the first one, we get\n\n$$\n5{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6(-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-6{e}^{-4t}+6{e}^{t}))+({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t}) \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2(-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}7{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t} & -7{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186222003 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(2t)={e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n2t{e}^{2t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(1-2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =3{e}^{2t}t+{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(1+t), \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{2t}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+t & t \\\\ -t & 1-t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186228893 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\alpha x+\\beta.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta) + (\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha x^2 + (2\\alpha+ \\beta)x + (\\beta+ \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=1, \\beta=0, \\gamma=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = x ^ 2 - 3.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186228894 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -2x + 5.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x + (\\alpha +2\\beta) = -2x + 5.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-1, \\,\\beta=3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = -x + 3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{-2x} - x + 3.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186228895 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 4y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha + 4(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha x + (\\alpha + 4\\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +4\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-2, \\,\\beta = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)= -2x + 2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-4x} -2x + 2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) = 1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-4(0)}-2(0)+2 & =1 \\\\ A+2 & =1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny \n=  -e^{-4x} - 2x + 2.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186228899 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\cos{x}.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos x + \\beta  \\sin x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =   - \\alpha \\sin {x} +  \\beta \\cos {x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n( - \\alpha \\sin {x} +  \\beta \\cos {x}) + 3(\\alpha \\cos x + \\beta  \\sin x) = 2\\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(3\\alpha+\\beta)\\cos x + (-\\alpha + 3\\beta) \\sin x = 2\\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac 3 5,$ $\\beta = \\dfrac 1 5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac{3}{5} \\cos(x) + \\dfrac{1}{5} \\sin(x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186228900 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 4 y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(3x) + \\beta  \\sin(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =   - 3\\alpha \\sin(3x) +  3\\beta \\cos(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2 ( - 3\\alpha \\sin(3x) +  3\\beta \\cos(3x)) + 4(\\alpha \\cos(3x)+ \\beta  \\sin(3x)) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta + 4\\alpha)\\cos(3x) + (-6\\alpha + 4 \\beta) \\sin(3x) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta +4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha +4\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=\\dfrac{11}{13}, \\beta=\\dfrac{10}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ny_p(x) =  \\dfrac{11}{13}\\cos(3x) + \\dfrac{10}{13} \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  A e^{-2x} + \\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{11}{13} }\\right)\\cos(3x) \n+ \n\\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{10}{13} }\\right) \\sin(3x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186228901 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 3y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $26 \\sin(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(2x) + \\beta  \\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' =   - 2\\alpha \\sin(2x) +  2\\beta \\cos(2x)\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n( - 2\\alpha \\sin(2x) +  2\\beta \\cos(2x)) + 3 (\\alpha \\cos(2x)+ \\beta  \\sin(2x)) = 26 \\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta+3\\alpha)\\cos(2x) + (-2\\alpha +3 \\beta) \\sin(2x) = 26 \\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta +3\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha +3\\beta =26\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-4, \\beta=6.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =  - 4\\cos(2x) + 6\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{-3x} - 4\\cos(2x) + 6\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =1 \\\\ A-4+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 5e^{-3x} - 4\\cos(2x) + 6\\sin(2x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186229623 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -12(\\alpha x+\\beta )=12x-13\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha x+(\\alpha -12\\beta )=12x-13.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -12\\beta =-13\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x+1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186229624 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x+\\beta )=4x+2\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x+(\\alpha +2\\beta )=4x+2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+2x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186229625 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -4(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x-4\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha x+(\\alpha -4\\beta )=8x-4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -4\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4(0)}-2(0)+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186229630 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-6\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +6\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3},\\beta =\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(-\\frac{1}{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(\\frac{1}{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186229640 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +\\beta =7\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{14}{5},\\beta =\\frac{7}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{14}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186229648 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of 