& -3 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=11,y=-6,z=-3.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186240895 "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+z=2 \\\\ 2y-3z=1 \\\\ 3x-y+2z=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4-3)-0+(0-6) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -9 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -9 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -9 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -9 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=1,y=2,z=1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186243890 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y-z=16 \\\\ -8x+4y+0z=-15 \\\\ 20x-y-3z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}9 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -1 & -3\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & 3 & -1 & 16 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0 & -15 \\\\ 20 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186243994 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}-11{x}_{3}=12\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186244042 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $a,b,c$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}a-4b-c=0 \\\\ -11a+0b+3c=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ -11 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -11 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186256747 "First, we organize the system so that it’s in the correct order and  both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x-y=-1 \\\\ x+y=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186262643 "The point $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+j+k \\\\ (3t+2)\\,i+(6t+1)\\,j+(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k & =2\\,i+j+k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3t+2=2 \\\\ 6t+1=1 \\\\ 1-4{t}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j-8t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =3\\,i+6\\,j-8(0)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,1,1)$ is $3\\,i+6\\,j.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186262931 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+0y=6 \\\\ 9x+4y=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186262948 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=5 \\\\ 5x-9y=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -9\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations: \n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -9 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186262953 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $a,b$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}a-6b=0 \\\\ 2a-7b=5 \\\\ 2a-4b=-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}\\frac{7}{2} & -6 \\\\ 2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}\\frac{7}{2} & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186262954 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $m,n$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}m+5n=11 \\\\ 0m-4n=-8 \\\\ 6m+n=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}\\frac{1}{3} & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}\\frac{1}{3} & 5 & 11 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 6 & 1 & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186265922 "First, notice that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation, all the variables are on the left-hand side, and all the constants are on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=4 \\\\ x-y=-10\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186265924 "First, we organize the system so that it’s in the correct order and  both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x-y=5 \\\\ -2x+y=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186265925 "First, we organize the system so that it’s in the correct order and  both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}0x+y=-3 \\\\ x+0y=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186265927 "The  $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3x-4y=-1 \\\\ 0x+y=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3x-4y=-1 \\\\ y=3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186265931 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-7x+0y+z=5 \\\\ -x+4y-3z=2 \\\\ x+2y+0z=6\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-3,\\,b=1,\\,c=6.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186266125 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}=1 \\\\ {e}^{-t}=1 \\\\ 2t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},-{e}^{-0},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},{e}^{-0},0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-2i+2j+2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{6\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186266126 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,0,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=1 \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0 \\\\ t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨-2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,-2{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0⟩⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-2i-2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186269104 "The given points are ${{(2,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$, ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(-1,4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (-1,4) & =(\\frac{2+a}{2},\\frac{2+b}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-1=\\frac{2+a}{2} \\\\ 4=\\frac{2+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}a=-4 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-4,6).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186272119 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A)=4+1=5.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186275140 "The given points are ${{(1,4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(3,5)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (3,5) & =(\\frac{1+a}{2},\\frac{4+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3=\\frac{1+a}{2} \\\\ 5=\\frac{4+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}a=5 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(5,6)$ and $a+b=11.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186281539 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1-2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1-2x=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}2x(1+2\\lambda )=1 \\\\ y(1-4\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,0),(1,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1+\\frac{1}{2})=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}),(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(\\frac{1}{3},\\pm \\frac{2}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\pm \\frac{2}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(-1,0)$ and its minimum value is $-2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186281541 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={y}^{2}-4x=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-4,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=-4\\lambda  \\\\ -4y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-2\\lambda  \\\\ y(\\lambda +2)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-4x=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x & =0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-2(-2)\\,⇒\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-4x=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-16 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(4,-4),(4,4).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,\\pm 4).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,\\pm 4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(4,\\pm 4)$ and its minimum value is $-16.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186281544 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+y=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4x=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1+\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ y=\\frac{\\lambda }{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,1).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation gives $y=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-\\frac{5}{4}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186281546 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-y=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ \\lambda =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & =1 \\\\ 1-y & =1 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(1,0).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens upwards, there isn't a maximum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the minimum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(1,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(1,0),$ and its minimum value is $-4.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186281550 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-2y=6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x-2,2y-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda (x-1) \\\\ 2y=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\lambda }{\\lambda -1} \\\\ y=\\frac{\\lambda }{\\lambda -1}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-2y & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x-2x & =6 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x & =6 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x-6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(3,3).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(-1,-1)$ | $(3,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $6$ | $22$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,0),$ and its minimum value is $4.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186281551 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $x=y=0$ which yields the critical point $(0,0).$ Notice that this point is not in the region's interior. However, it is in the boundary of the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2(x-1),2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8x=2\\lambda (x-1) \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}4x=\\lambda (x-1) \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=1\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(2,0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n4x=-(x-1)\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{5}-1)}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{3}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(\\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(\\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{3}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $16$ | $-\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,0),$ and its maximum value is $16.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186281552 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $x=y=0$ which yields the critical point $(0,0).$ Notice that this point is not in the region's interior. However, it is in the boundary of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2(y-1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=8\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-4\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y=-\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=1\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4}(y-1)\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{5}-1)}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5})\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-4.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186292874 "First, we organize the system so that it is in the correct order and  both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3x-4y=8 \\\\ 2x+0y=-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299608 "First, notice that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation, all the variables are on the left-hand side, and all the constants are on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y=10 \\\\ 5x+2y=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299609 "First, let's write the system so that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation. \nThe variable $x$ does not appear in the first equation, but this just means its coefficient is $0.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}0x-6y=4 \\\\ 3x+5y\\,=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299615 "First, we organize the system so that it is in the correct order and  both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=-5 \\\\ 3x-2y=7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299617 "First, we organize the system so that it is in the correct order and both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=9 \\\\ 0x-5y=-15\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299618 "First, we organize the system so that it is in the correct order and both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x+4y=3 \\\\ 2x+9y=5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299619 "First, we organize the system so that it’s in the correct order and both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+0y=4 \\\\ 0x+y=-10\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299622 "The $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4x-5y=11 \\\\ -3x+2y=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299623 "The  $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}0x+1y=5 \\\\ -1x+0y=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y=5 \\\\ -x=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186299624 "The  $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5x+0y=10 \\\\ -3x+2y=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5x=10 \\\\ -3x+2y=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186304794 "The point $P(2,4,8)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{0}$ corresponds to the value of $t$ at the point $P.$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k \\\\ \\sqrt{1+t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-5)\\,j+(3t-1)\\,k & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+t}=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-5=4 \\\\ 3t-1=8\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the third equation, we get $t=3.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,4,8)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(3).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1+t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-5)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1+t}}\\,i+2t\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1+(3)}}\\,i+2(3)\\,j+3\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}\\,i+2\\cdot 3\\,j+3\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(2,4,8)$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186304829 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\pi \\,i+\\,j+0\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =2\\pi \\,i+\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=2\\pi  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t=1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=2\\pi .$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(2\\pi ).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2\\pi :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2\\pi ) & =\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =i+0\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =i+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(2\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(2\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+0+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(2\\pi )$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2\\pi ,1,0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2\\pi ) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(2\\pi )}{‖{r}^{′}(2\\pi )‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot (i+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186304902 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,4,8)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\,k & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=4 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}=8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=2.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2,4,8)$ is at the tip of $r(2).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+4t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\,j+4\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =i+4\\,j+8\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2,4,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(2)}{‖{r}^{′}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (i+4\\,j+8\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\,i+\\frac{4}{9}\\,j+\\frac{8}{9}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186304904 "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(4,0,1)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨4,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4t=4 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t=0 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(4,0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{4}{t},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{4}{(1)},\\,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(4,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨4,\\,4,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186304956 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x+4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(2\\beta +2\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x+4.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta +2\\gamma =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0,\\gamma =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186304959 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\beta -2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha -2\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta -2\\gamma =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =-\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186305015 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +8(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x+9\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n8\\alpha x+(\\alpha +8\\beta )=8x+9.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +8\\beta =9\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x+1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186305016 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -4(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-4x-7\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha x+(\\alpha -4\\beta )=-4x-7.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -4\\beta =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x+2.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186305017 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-6x-7\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha x+(\\alpha +6\\beta )=-6x-7.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +6\\beta =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x-1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186305019 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186305021 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x-2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =0,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}-2.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186305030 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=9x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\alpha x+(\\alpha -3\\beta )=9x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha =9\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -3\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$