ned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{11}{50},\\beta =\\frac{13}{50}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{11}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\frac{13}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(\\frac{11}{50})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(\\frac{13}{50})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186305139 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-8{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta -8\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\beta -8\\alpha =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -8\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{13},\\beta =-\\frac{19}{52}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{19}{52}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(\\frac{1}{13})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-\\frac{19}{52})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308351 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta -\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-2\\alpha -\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta -\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha -\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-4,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{1\\cdot 0}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =3 \\\\ A-4-0 & =3 \\\\ A & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=7{e}^{x}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308352 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta +3\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-2\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta +3\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha +3\\beta =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =1 \\\\ A-2-0 & =1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n