functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(3t)={e}^{3t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{e}^{3t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(1-3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2{e}^{3t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(-2t) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-{e}^{3t}t+{e}^{3t}(1-3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(1-4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+4t & -2t \\\\ 8t & 1-4t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186308675 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\nt{e}^{t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(1-t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6{e}^{t}t+{e}^{t}(1-t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(1+5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5{e}^{t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+5t & -5t \\\\ 5t & 1-5t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311937 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{4x})({e}^{-4x})^{′}-({e}^{4x})^{′}({e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{4x})(-4{e}^{-4x})-(4{e}^{4x})({e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =-4-4 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{4x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-4x})}^{′}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-4x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{8x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{8x}){e}^{-4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 8{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int 2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311938 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(-{e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{-x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}){e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186311939 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(-{e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{-x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{-x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,(-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}){e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =-\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186335695 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ So we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =0$ and $\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186335697 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & =8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+16x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\,=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-8\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+8\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8\\beta =16\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ -8\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1$ and $\\beta =2.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186335765 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2}.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {t}^{3}+\\beta {t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are the constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=3\\alpha {t}^{2}+2\\beta t,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=6\\alpha t+2\\beta .\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}(6\\alpha t+2\\beta )+2t(3\\alpha {t}^{2}+2\\beta t) & =12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2} \\\\ (6\\alpha +6\\alpha ){t}^{3}+(2\\beta +4\\beta ){t}^{2} & =12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2} \\\\ 12\\alpha {t}^{3}+6\\beta {t}^{2} & =12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{t}^{3}) \\\\ 6\\beta =-24\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\,\\beta =-4.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186335766 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+6y=12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{2t}+B{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha +6(\\alpha t+\\beta )=12t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha t+(-5\\alpha +6\\beta )=12t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -5\\alpha +6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =\\frac{5}{3},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186338975 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+0z=3 \\\\ 0x-4y+5z=6 \\\\ 7x+0y+8z=9\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186338976 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=3 \\\\ 2x+y=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{c|c}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations: \n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186338990 "First, notice that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation, all the variables are on the left-hand side, and all the constants are on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=7 \\\\ x+y=-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186338992 "First, let's write the system so that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation. \nThe variable $y$ does not appear in the second equation, but this just means its coefficient is $0.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x+5y=-1 \\\\ 5x+0y=15\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186341850 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=0 \\\\ 2x=3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186341853 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x-3y=0 \\\\ x+y=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186342176 "Given that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2)+a\\cdot 1+b\\cdot 5+5\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ -4+a+5b-10 & =0 \\\\ a+5b & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1+a\\cdot 4+b\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ 2+4a-b+5 & =0 \\\\ 4a-b & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}a+5b=14 \\\\ 4a-b=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-4$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-21b=-63\\,⟹\\,b=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+5(3) & =14 \\\\ a+15 & =14 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na-b=-1-3=-4.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186345090 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y-3z=2 \\\\ 3x-6y+0z=5 \\\\ 2x-3y+5z=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,\\,b=0,\\,c=-3.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186345093 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}0x-2y+3z=7 \\\\ x+0y+z=-1 \\\\ -2x+y+3z=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=0,\\,b=0,\\,c=3.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186345094 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+5y-3z=2 \\\\ 7x-2y+3z=5 \\\\ 2x-2y+0z=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,\\,b=-2,\\,c=0.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186345098 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5x+2y+3z=-1 \\\\ x-3y+0z=3 \\\\ -x-3y+2z=-5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,\\,b=0,\\,c=-1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186345099 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y+z=-1 \\\\ 3x-2y+z=3 \\\\ -x-y+2z=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-1,\\,b=-2,\\,c=2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186345100 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x-5y-2z=1 \\\\ -4x-y+5z=-2 \\\\ 2x-6y-z=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-2,\\,b=-4,\\,c=-6.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186345101 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5x+2y+z=0 \\\\ 2x+0y+z=1 \\\\ -3x+7y-2z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,\\,b=1,\\,c=-2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186348148 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =1$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+2\\alpha -3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=3x+1\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-3\\alpha x+(2\\alpha -3\\beta )=3x+1.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -3\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-x-1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186351217 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-5y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-5$ or $\\lambda =1$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)-5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n(-14\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(-14\\beta -12\\alpha )\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-14\\alpha +12\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -14\\beta -12\\alpha =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{10},\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-\\frac{2}{5}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{10}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{10}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-5A+B=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -5A+B=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0,$ $B=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186351232 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{3}{4}\\alpha -\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(\\alpha -\\frac{3}{4}\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}\\alpha -\\beta =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\alpha -\\frac{3}{4}\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-12$ and $\\beta =-16.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186351238 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -2)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =2$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $13\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)-4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-5\\alpha -12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(12\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha -12\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ 12\\alpha -5\\beta =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{12}{13}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{5}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186351241 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+31\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+2(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+31\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(-6\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+31\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +6\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -6\\alpha +\\beta =31\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-5$ and $\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186351501 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =10-24 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186351506 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,0,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=0 \\\\ 2t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =2j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186354441 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{2})\\cdot 2-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =-7\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186354631 "First, notice that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation, all the variables are on the left-hand side, and all the constants are on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6x+4y=1 \\\\ x-8y=-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186357569 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-16(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x-32\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-16\\alpha x-16\\beta =8x-32.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-16\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -16\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{x}{2}+2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}-\\frac{x}{2}+2.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186357573 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ So we assume the particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-8\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-8\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ -8\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,$ $\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186357574 "The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+5x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =10(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x)-25x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n10(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x)-25x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+25x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)=-20\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-10\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+10\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡5x=-20\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}10\\beta =-20\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x) \\\\ -10\\alpha =-10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =-2.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186357577 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =-3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +7(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=24{x}^{2}+4x+2\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(14\\alpha +12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +7\\beta +12\\gamma )=24{x}^{2}+4x+2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha =24\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 14\\alpha +12\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +7\\beta +12\\gamma =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}-2x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by \n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}-2x+1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186357642 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is  $2{x}^{2}+1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are the constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\alpha )+2x(2\\alpha x)-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta ) & =2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ (2\\alpha +4\\alpha -12\\alpha ){x}^{2}-12\\beta  & =2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ -6\\alpha {x}^{2}-12\\beta  & =2{x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ -12\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\,\\beta =-\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186357657 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is  $2x-3.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are the constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+2x(\\alpha )-6(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =2x-3 \\\\ (2\\alpha -6\\alpha )x-6\\beta  & =2x-3 \\\\ -4\\alpha x-6\\beta  & =2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -6\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186357658 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is  $5x-2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are the constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+4x(\\alpha )-16(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =5x-2 \\\\ (4\\alpha -16\\alpha )x-16\\beta  & =5x-2 \\\\ -12\\alpha x-16\\beta  & =5x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -16\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{5}{12},\\,\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{5x}{12}+\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186357690 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-7y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and sinusoidal forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=\\frac{A}{{e}^{7t}}+B{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (-\\alpha +6\\beta -7\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-\\beta -6\\alpha -7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (-8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-6\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha +6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ -6\\alpha -8\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{25}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{4}{25},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186357691 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+B{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-3\\alpha +2(\\alpha t+\\beta )=3t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha t+2\\beta -3\\alpha =3t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ 2\\beta -3\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the constants}).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{9}{4},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3t}{2}+\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186357692 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y=t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+Bt{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-2\\alpha +(\\alpha t+\\beta )=t+2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha t+\\beta -2\\alpha =t+2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ \\beta -2\\alpha =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants}).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =4,$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t+4.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186360768 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x+y+2z=1 \\\\ 3x+6y-2z=2 \\\\ x-y+3z=-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-1,\\,b=6,\\,c=-3.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186363465 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x+1,2y+1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global maximum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=3\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x+1,2y+1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x+1=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2(\\lambda -1)} \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2(\\lambda -1)}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $3-\\sqrt{6}$ | $3+\\sqrt{6}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $3+\\sqrt{6}.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186363472 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨20x,-10y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $x=y=0$ which yields the critical point $(0,0).$ Notice that this point is not in the region's interior. However, it is in the boundary of the parabola ${x}^{2}-y=0.$\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-y=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨20x,-10y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}20x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -10y=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(10-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ 10y=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-y & =0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =10$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n10y=10\\,⇒\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 1,1).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(\\pm 1,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\pm 1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-3$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\pm 1,1),$ and its maximum value is $2.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186363500 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-2z=2,\\,h(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,0,-2⟩+\\mu ⟨0,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda  \\\\ 1=2\\mu y \\\\ -2=-2\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda =1 \\\\ 1=2\\mu y \\\\ \\mu z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.  So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0.$ \n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$ into the first constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2z & =2 \\\\ x-0 & =2 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute  $z=0$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2,1,0),\\,(2,-1,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2,-1,0)$ | $(2,1,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(2,-1,0),$ and its minimum value is $1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186363507 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3x+2y+z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+2y+z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,2,1⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,4y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 2=2\\lambda +4\\mu y \\\\ 1=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{4\\mu } \\\\ \\mu y=0 \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the second equation above, we must have $y=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $y=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}+2(0)+z & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}+z & =1 \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}+2(0)+z & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}+z & =1 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{3}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ and its  minimum value is $0.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186363548 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve  ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-2,\\,2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,0).$ Since the point $(1,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-3,0)$ and $(0,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-3,0).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}+{t}^{2}-2t+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-3,0),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}-2t+1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution, $t=1$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (-3,0).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,3)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,3).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2(0)+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,3),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution, $t=0$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (0,3).$\n\n- The quarter-circle connecting $(-3,0)$ and $(0,3)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\,t\\in (-3,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{t}^{2}-2t+1 \\\\  & =9-{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}-2t+1 \\\\  & =10-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-3,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, we obtain three candidates on the boundary:\n\n$$\n(-3,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(0,3)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $16$ | $1$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $1$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $16$ at the point $(-3,0).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186363719 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(2\\beta +2\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta +2\\gamma =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =1,\\gamma =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186363726 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=12x+8\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n6\\alpha x+(\\alpha +6\\beta )=12x+8\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +6\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x+1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x}+2x+1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186364934 "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(16,12,16)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨16,12,16⟩ \\\\ ⟨8t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{3}⟩ & =⟨16,12,16⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8t=16 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}=12 \\\\ 2{t}^{3}=16.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=2.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(16,12,16)$ is at the tip of $r(2).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,6t,\\,6{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =⟨8,\\,6(2),\\,6(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,12,24⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2,3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(2)‖ & =4\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{4+9+36} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(16,12,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(2)}{‖{r}^{′}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{28}\\cdot 4⟨2,3,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{7},\\frac{3}{7},\\frac{6}{7}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186365213 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2x,-6y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x=4\\lambda x \\\\ -6y=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1+2\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +3)=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y & =0\\,⇒\\,y=0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-\\frac{1}{2}+3)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}=-\\frac{11}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,0)$ and its maximum value is $1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186365617 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +4(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-4x-5\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha x+(\\alpha +4\\beta )=-4x-5\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +4\\beta =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x-1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}-x-1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186365627 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=12x+8\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha x+(\\alpha -6\\beta )=12x+8\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -6\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6x}-2x-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6(0)}-2(0)-\\frac{5}{3} & =2 \\\\ A-\\frac{5}{3} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{11}{3}\\,{e}^{6x}-2x-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186365673 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t)={e}^{-4t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(-4t)={e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{e}^{-4t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}(1+4t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-4{e}^{-4t}t+{e}^{-4t}(1+4t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{-4t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t} & t{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186366184 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+B{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-3\\alpha +2(\\alpha t+\\beta )=t-4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha t+(-3\\alpha +2\\beta )=t-4.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -3\\alpha +2\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{5}{4},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t-\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{2t-5}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5}{4}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186366574 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-10y=5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=\\frac{A}{{e}^{5t}}+B{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0+3\\alpha -10(\\alpha t+\\beta )=5t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-10\\alpha t-10\\beta +3\\alpha =t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-10\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -10\\beta +3\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the constants}).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{3}{20},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186370129 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & -{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot \\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186370138 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\nt{e}^{t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(1-t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3{e}^{t}t, \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{t}t+{e}^{t}(1-t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186371457 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x-2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x-2y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2y)^{2}+y=2\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v=2\\,⟹\\,v=2-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=2-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $(\\pm 1,1).$\n\n![](images/21/212067104713e1b70eb6ee0684d15fa77d4dbf3a732b6ddba614ed9e12c4bccd.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2-{u}^{2}}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186371471 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3x+{y}^{2} \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $3x+{y}^{2}=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+{y}^{2})^{2}+y=5\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v=5\\,⟹\\,v=5-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\,⟹\\,v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=5-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ v=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{5},0).$\n\n![](images/f6/f667b68a08f6897af0116778f5f21c5b8b0d1ce6aff09e315431dc445d6998bb.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)={(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{5}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,0\\le v\\le 5-{u}^{2}}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186372628 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+10{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(10\\alpha +15\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-15\\alpha +10\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}10\\alpha +15\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -15\\alpha +10\\beta =7\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{21}{65},\\beta =\\frac{14}{65}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{21}{65}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{14}{65}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+(-\\frac{21}{65})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(\\frac{14}{65})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186372633 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n7\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+21{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n7(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+21(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(21\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-21\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}21\\alpha +21\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -21\\alpha +21\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{42},\\beta =\\frac{5}{42}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{5}{42}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{5}{42}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+(\\frac{5}{42})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(\\frac{5}{42})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186372638 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(\\beta -2\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha -2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\beta -2\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha -2\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{3}{5},$ $\\beta =\\frac{6}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186373298 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x-y \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v-1$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+y=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v-1=3\\,⟹\\,v=4-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v-1$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,v-1=2\\,⟹\\,v=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=4-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,3).$\n\n![](images/4d/4d719cc14094ad632069bcdb9307929bae3030072c1ce3d0f57f3ca682e0aac0.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,3\\le v\\le 4-{u}^{2}}.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186373311 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y+1\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v+1$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+y=2\\,⟹\\,(y+1)=3-(x-y)^{2}\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhile substituting $y=v-1$ into the equation of the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=3-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,1\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{2}.}.\n$$\n\n![](images/9c/9cec7ff29557aa14cd19309e9180482a44091e7ef215205285822674e7bc25b0.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{3-{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{1}^{3-{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(3-{u}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-2\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186376714 "First, we recall the definition of an injection:\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if every element $x\\in X$ is mapped to a distinct element in $Y:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\ne {x}_{2}\\,⇒\\,f({x}_{1})\\ne f({x}_{2})\n$$\n\nIn this case, it would be easier to prove the contrapositive statement. \n\nThis implication is equivalent to its contrapositive:\n\n$$\nf({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2})\\,⇒\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we simply need to derive ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$ by assuming that $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}=({z}_{1},{w}_{1})$ and ${x}_{2}=({z}_{2},{w}_{2}):$\n\nSo, let $({z}_{1},{w}_{1}),({z}_{2},{w}_{2})\\in C\\times C,$ and suppose that $f({z}_{1},{w}_{1})=f({z}_{2},{w}_{2}).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({z}_{1},{w}_{1}) & =f({z}_{2},{w}_{2}) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{{z}_{1}-{w}_{1}},{z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{1}) & =(\\overset{―}{{z}_{2}-{w}_{2}},{z}_{2}\\text{i}+{w}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two pairs are equal if and only if their respective components are equal, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{z}_{1}-{w}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{{z}_{2}-{w}_{2}} \\\\ {z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{1}={z}_{2}\\text{i}+{w}_{2}\\end{aligned} & \\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}-{w}_{1}={z}_{2}-{w}_{2} \\\\ {z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{1}={z}_{2}\\text{i}+{w}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,⇒\\,{z}_{1}+{z}_{1}\\text{i}={z}_{2}+{z}_{2}\\text{i} \\\\  & \\,⇒\\,{z}_{1}(1+\\text{i})={z}_{2}(1+\\text{i}) \\\\  & \\,⇒\\,{z}_{1}={z}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{1} & ={z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{2} \\\\ {w}_{1} & ={w}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state the conclusion:\n\nHence, $({z}_{1},{w}_{1})=({z}_{2},{w}_{2}),$ as was to be shown. Therefore, $f$ is an injection."] [#edb/ref 17592186377132 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n7\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-14{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n7(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-14(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(14\\beta -14\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-14\\alpha -14\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}14\\beta -14\\alpha =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -14\\alpha -14\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{28},\\beta =-\\frac{11}{28}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{5}{28}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{11}{28}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(\\frac{5}{28})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-\\frac{11}{28})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186385954 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y=9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+B{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha +3(\\alpha t+\\beta )=9t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n3\\alpha t+(3\\beta -4\\alpha )=9t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha =9\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ 3\\beta -4\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =3$ and $\\beta =4,$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=3t+4.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186389360 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y-2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,1).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=9\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y-2=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda -1)=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, this doesn't give any critical point.\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation gives $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =9\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{37}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{37}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{37}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(\\frac{\\pm \\sqrt{37}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{17}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,1),$ and its minimum value is $-1.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186389367 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y-z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4=2\\mu x \\\\ 1=\\lambda  \\\\ -1=-\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =1 \\\\ \\mu z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$ into the first constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-z & =1 \\\\ y-0 & =1 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute  $z=0$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2,1,0),\\,(-2,1,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2,1,0)$ | $(-2,1,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $9$ | $-7$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,1,0),$ and its maximum value is $9.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186389374 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2x+3y+2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints  \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2x-y-z=-1,\\,h(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=-1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,3,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨0,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda  \\\\ 3=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 2=-\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda =1 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{\\mu } \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{2\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $y$ and $z$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,z=\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $y=\\frac{4}{5}$ and $z=\\frac{3}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y-z & =-1 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5} & =-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}. \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $y=-\\frac{4}{5}$ and $z=-\\frac{3}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y-z & =-1 \\\\ 2x-(-\\frac{4}{5})-(-\\frac{3}{5}) & =-1 \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{12}{5} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}),\\,(-\\frac{6}{5},-\\frac{4}{5},-\\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5})$ | $(-\\frac{6}{5},-\\frac{4}{5},-\\frac{3}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $4$ | $-6$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}),$ and its maximum value is $4.$"] [#edb/ref 17592186399180 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y-1,\\,x-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y-1=0 \\\\ x-1=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,1).$ Since the point $(1,1)$ is not in the interior of $D$,  we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the line $y=2-x,$ in the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)(0)-(t)-(0) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)(t)-(0)-(t) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨0,2⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t⟨-2,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2-2t,2t) \\\\  & =(2-2t)(2t)-(2-2t)-(2t) \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}+4t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (-4{t}^{2}+4t-2)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8t+4 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point $(1,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(2,0),\\,(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the point $(0,0).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186401579 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2x,\\,2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 32\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-15$ | $17$ | $-15$ | $17$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-15$ at the points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the points $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186403495 "The  $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}0x-y=0 \\\\ -3x+12y=-15.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ x-4y=5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186404357 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}6-2y,\\,-2x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6-2y=0 \\\\ -2x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,3).$ Since the point $(0,3)$ does not belong to the closed region of $D,$ we do not include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =6(t)-2(t)(0) \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =6(t)-2(t)(2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3}+2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation has no solution inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$ So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain two candidates:\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-12$ | $12$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-12$ at the point $(-2,0)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $12$ at the point $(2,0).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186404364 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo do that, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4y,\\,-4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4y=0 \\\\ -4x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ -18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(3,0)$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-8$ | $10$ | $-8$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the points $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $10$ at the points $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}).$"] [#edb/ref 17592186404586 "The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)+6x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =12(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)-36x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n12(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)-36x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)+36x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+12\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡6x=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\beta =-12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x) \\\\ -12\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =0$ and $\\beta =-1.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186404593 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+4(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha +4\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta +4\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha +4\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta +4\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+1.\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186405125 "The point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =-i+0\\,j+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j+t\\,k & =-i+0\\,j+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t=-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t=0 \\\\ t=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $t=\\frac{3}{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{3}{2}).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{3}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+\\pi \\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u\\,(\\pi \\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"] [#edb/ref 17592186409494 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=12x-8\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha x+(\\alpha -6\\beta )=12x-8.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -6\\beta =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+1.\n$$"]}
